1
00:00:01,810 --> 00:00:04,585
This is the technical walkthrough of Backstop:

2
00:00:04,585 --> 00:00:09,318
a CashScript vault that pre-funds a rescue for a ParyonUSD loan.

3
00:00:10,522 --> 00:00:12,186
There are four pieces.

4
00:00:12,186 --> 00:00:13,850
The borrower's wallet.

5
00:00:13,850 --> 00:00:17,302
The vault, which is two outputs at one contract address:

6
00:00:17,302 --> 00:00:23,485
a key output holding Paryon's minting loan key, and a reserve output holding plain BCH.

7
00:00:23,485 --> 00:00:27,941
They are separate because Paryon's manage function requires the loan key

8
00:00:27,941 --> 00:00:31,180
to come back with exactly the value it went in with.

9
00:00:31,180 --> 00:00:34,590
Then Paryon's own contracts: the price contract,

10
00:00:34,590 --> 00:00:36,424
the loan with its sidecar,

11
00:00:36,424 --> 00:00:38,171
and the manage function.

12
00:00:38,171 --> 00:00:42,249
And the keeper: a stateless process with no keys and no funds.

13
00:00:44,054 --> 00:00:46,353
Setup is one wallet signature.

14
00:00:46,353 --> 00:00:49,424
The loan key and the reserve move into the vault,

15
00:00:49,424 --> 00:00:52,428
and an OP_RETURN records every vault parameter.

16
00:00:52,428 --> 00:00:57,031
So anyone can rebuild the vault's address and rules from chain data alone.

17
00:00:57,031 --> 00:01:00,036
The keeper and the web app need no database.

18
00:01:01,841 --> 00:01:07,422
The rescue is Paryon's own add-collateral transaction, manage with no repayment,

19
00:01:07,422 --> 00:01:09,916
with the vault at inputs 4 and 5.

20
00:01:09,916 --> 00:01:15,607
Paryon fixes inputs and outputs 0 to 4 and leaves index 5 onward free,

21
00:01:15,607 --> 00:01:19,274
which is where the reserve and an optional keeper reward go.

22
00:01:19,274 --> 00:01:20,764
Nobody signs.

23
00:01:20,764 --> 00:01:24,503
The price comes from Paryon's price contract at input 0,

24
00:01:24,503 --> 00:01:31,435
which only accepts oracle-signed messages, verified with checkDataSig, with an increasing sequence number,

25
00:01:31,435 --> 00:01:34,120
so an old low price cannot be replayed.

26
00:01:35,656 --> 00:01:39,305
Every guarantee is a require in Backstop.cash.

27
00:01:39,305 --> 00:01:42,580
The rescue path checks the exact transaction shape;

28
00:01:42,580 --> 00:01:45,307
that the vault sits at positions 4 and 5;

29
00:01:45,307 --> 00:01:47,532
a relative timelock cooldown;

30
00:01:47,532 --> 00:01:49,558
the genuine price contract;

31
00:01:49,558 --> 00:01:55,233
that the loan belongs to this key, and its debt, state and interest manager are unchanged;

32
00:01:55,234 --> 00:01:57,716
that the manage function is Paryon's;

33
00:01:57,716 --> 00:02:00,794
that the top-up is positive and within the limit;

34
00:02:00,794 --> 00:02:04,125
that the key comes straight back with its minting capability;

35
00:02:04,125 --> 00:02:08,351
that the reserve returns, minus at most the top-up and a capped fee;

36
00:02:08,351 --> 00:02:14,680
and the ratio rules: below the trigger, never above the target, and no draining through tiny rescues.

37
00:02:14,680 --> 00:02:18,515
The ratio maths is cross-multiplied, with no division.

38
00:02:20,062 --> 00:02:23,173
There are two ways out, both only for the borrower.

39
00:02:23,173 --> 00:02:26,013
ownerSpend checks the owner's key and signature.

40
00:02:26,013 --> 00:02:31,090
ownerWithdraw exists because WizardConnect wallets sign only their own coins:

41
00:02:31,090 --> 00:02:35,163
the owner spends one of their own coins in the same transaction,

42
00:02:35,163 --> 00:02:38,214
the contract checks it sits at the owner's address,

43
00:02:38,214 --> 00:02:40,295
every output pays the owner,

44
00:02:40,295 --> 00:02:41,875
and the fee is capped.

45
00:02:43,092 --> 00:02:48,592
Each rescue attack is tested twice: against Paryon's real compiled contracts,

46
00:02:48,592 --> 00:02:52,129
and with Paryon's checks replaced by permissive stand-ins,

47
00:02:52,129 --> 00:02:54,900
where it must fail on Backstop's own require,

48
00:02:54,900 --> 00:02:56,732
matched by its message.

49
00:02:56,732 --> 00:03:00,610
So no guarantee depends on Paryon catching the same mistake.

50
00:03:00,610 --> 00:03:05,962
The suite runs 164 tests, including the full demo on an in-memory

51
00:03:05,962 --> 00:03:12,125
node that enforces relative timelocks, standardness and fees the way BCHN does.

52
00:03:12,125 --> 00:03:16,793
And a mutation test switches off each of the fifty-five requires in turn:

53
00:03:16,793 --> 00:03:21,845
fifty-four are caught by a named test, and the last one duplicates another check.

54
00:03:24,253 --> 00:03:31,204
And it ran on chipnet, with Paryon's unmodified loan, sidecar, manage and price contract bytecode.

55
00:03:31,204 --> 00:03:33,723
Here is the setup, the price update,

56
00:03:33,878 --> 00:03:36,802
the rescue sent by the keeper, and the withdraw.

57
00:03:36,802 --> 00:03:41,389
The setup and the withdraw were signed in Cashonize over WizardConnect.

58
00:03:43,250 --> 00:03:44,973
The known limitations.

59
00:03:44,973 --> 00:03:50,794
On chipnet the price comes from a demo oracle key; mainnet would use General Protocols.

60
00:03:50,794 --> 00:03:54,886
A stale price means no rescue, but also no liquidation.

61
00:03:54,886 --> 00:04:00,238
A crash faster than any keeper, or within the cooldown, can still be liquidated first.

62
00:04:00,238 --> 00:04:03,776
Redemptions are out of scope, and there is one loan per vault.

63
00:04:03,776 --> 00:04:08,935
Backstop reduces liquidation risk without asking the borrower to trust anyone.
